  <section class="section container">
    <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Jebel Ali is the Middle East re-export and consumer-goods hub backed by JAFZA; Khalifa Port is Abu Dhabi’s deep-water industrial gateway backed by KIZAD.</li>
      <li>Jebel Ali ≈14.5m TEU is a throughput figure, while Khalifa’s ≈9m+ TEU is a design capacity — do not compare them as identical metrics.</li>
      <li>Khalifa has the deeper draft, ≈18 m versus ≈17 m, and the profile’s less-congested, project-cargo positioning.</li>
      <li>For non-UAE destinations, Jebel Ali is often only the first leg — price the onward feeder or truck, not just the ocean freight.</li>
      <li>No freight rate, transit or certification number is invented here — confirm free zone versus mainland treatment and quote the full destination before booking.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>The Author's Take</h2>
    <p><strong>Position:</strong> I would not send Abu Dhabi-local or project cargo to Jebel Ali by default, and I would not send pure re-export consumer goods to Khalifa Port by default. Jebel Ali’s edge is JAFZA distribution and regional reach; Khalifa Port’s edge is draft, space and KIZAD industrial fit.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Draft gap is 18 − 17 = 1 m, but for heavy or project cargo the profile says Khalifa often wins on draft and space.</li>
      <li>Jebel Ali ≈14.5m TEU versus Khalifa design capacity ≈9m+ TEU is a scale signal, not a like-for-like cost comparison.</li>
      <li>The Jebel Ali profile warns that an arrival there is often only the first leg for non-UAE final buyers, so the onward cost must be added.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note"><em>Disclosure: This is the author's editorial judgment based on the published data on this page — not an independently verified fact.</em></p>
